### 2.3. API Loading and Error State Management

Managing the asynchronous nature of API requests fundamentally requires handling loading states (to indicate ongoing operations) and error states (to inform the user of failures). `react-query` inherently provides these states (`isLoading`, `isError`, `error`).

Error handling is also observed through event-driven updates. Real-time events from the backend (e.g., `"message.updated"`) are used to directly update the `react-query` cache via `queryClient.setQueryData`, ensuring the UI reflects the latest state without full data refetches.

The `openapi-types.ts` file defines specific error schemas (`ProviderAuthError`, `UnknownError`, `MessageOutputLengthError`, `MessageAbortedError`), which are referenced within `Event.session.error`. This structured approach to errors allows the frontend to react to specific failure conditions with tailored UI feedback.

